chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:38:<p>The obligation is old, not an approaching regulatory wave. Dutch NVWA guidance says durability information must be readable and findable; highly perishable foods use a use-by date and other foods a minimum-durability or best-before date. <!-- CHECK: 03-date-duty --> EU rules separately require a lot indication for food, with specified exemptions, and place it on the prepackaging or attached label when food is prepacked. <!-- CHECK: 03-lot-duty --> General Food Law requires traceability systems and requires a food business operator that believes food is unsafe to initiate withdrawal procedures once it has left the operator’s immediate control. <!-- CHECK: 03-trace-withdraw --> None of those texts says “buy machine vision.” They establish the consequence and leave the control method to the operator.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:39:<p>The failure is real. In December 2025 the NVWA published a safety warning after one retailer sold pita packs without a durability date and wraps printed with 5 December 2026 instead of 5 December 2025. <!-- CHECK: 03-real-warning --> That is evidence that missing and wrong dates can pass through production and reach consumers. It is not evidence that Dutch cheese packers will spend €52,000 on this proposed cell. <!-- CHECK: 03-no-budget-proof --> Payment has to be discovered.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:40:<p>The practical change is that the component stack has become product-like. Cognex currently presents In-Sight 3800 as able to read food-and-beverage lot and expiry codes on uneven surfaces; its documentation lists global-shutter monochrome sensors, discrete I/O, Ethernet and IP67 protection when correctly assembled. <!-- CHECK: 03-cognex-ocr --><!-- CHECK: 03-cognex-spec --> Domino’s R-Series packages code validation with preconfigured cameras and lights, while Videojet-Laetus combines OCR/OCV, printer synchronisation and SKU control. <!-- CHECK: 03-domino-range --><!-- CHECK: 03-videojet-system --> These are not proof that the proposed cheese module wins. They mean the founder need not invent OCR—and cannot claim OCR as a moat.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:48:<p>The initial screen contains ten names: Royal A-ware, Vergeer Holland, Vandersterre, CONO Kaasmakers, FrieslandCampina, Royal Lactalis Leerdammer, Bouter Cheese, Kaas-Pack Holland, Henri Willig and Vepo Cheese. This is an outreach list, not a claim that any has an unprotected matching line or buying intent. <!-- CHECK: 03-prospect-list --> A partner first checks public pack format and its own installed-base knowledge, then asks the plant operations manager one question: “Show us the last code-related hold, customer complaint or audit action, and what it cost.” Five conversations with no dated event end the segment test early.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:49:<p>Acquisition should begin through one Dutch packaging or vision integrator, not by the English-speaking founder cold-calling factory floors. The first named channel test is Vision Partners: ask whether it will put a white-label cheese-code module inside EVIS while retaining the mechanics, PLC and customer scope. EVIS’s published integrations make the overlap—and therefore the risk of a quick no—explicit. <!-- CHECK: 03-evis-channel --> The partner owns the Dutch introduction and safety conversation; the founder can conduct the technical workshop in English and build B1/B2 Dutch for commissioning. Zaandam is useful for travel, not a sales advantage. A business that still needs the founder to interpret rapid Dutch operator discussion at customer twenty has failed its own scale test.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:79:<p>All volumes, prices and costs are designed arithmetic, not market facts. <!-- CHECK: 03-prices --> Year one is intentionally a loss after charging the founder’s labour: it buys proof, not owner income. The mature design pays €100,000 for the founder role and retains €74,000 before company tax. That clears the reader’s income gate without pretending the owner works free.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:88:<p>The PLC—not cloud software—tracks the pack from camera to reject. A pusher or air blast removes it; a downstream sensor confirms departure; bin-full, missed trigger, unconfirmed reject or encoder discrepancy stops the line. The integrator owns that deterministic state machine, guarding and restart behaviour. From 20 January 2027 the EU Machinery Regulation applies, and a party making a substantial modification can be treated as the manufacturer with conformity obligations for the affected machinery. <!-- CHECK: 03-machinery-date --><!-- CHECK: 03-substantial-mod --> That is why the vision company does not casually splice into a safety circuit or claim that a CE-marked camera makes a CE-compliant cell.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:89:<p>Acceptance starts with a jointly signed defect taxonomy: missing print; stale or future date; transposed digit; incomplete character; weak contrast; smear; double print; displacement; glare; wrinkle; condensation; wrong lot format. The factory test runs 3,000 labelled defect images with zero misses, then a site test runs at least 100 physical challenge packs through trigger, decision, tracking and confirmed reject. A 10,000-good-pack production run must stay below the agreed false-reject rate—initial hurdle 0.2%—without bypass. Zero misses in an image library does not prove zero field risk, especially when images from one run are correlated. <!-- CHECK: 03-stat-test --> Results are reported by film, ribbon, line speed and defect class, not as one flattering accuracy percentage.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:90:<p>Cleaning belongs in the design review. EU hygiene rules require relevant equipment and its installation to permit cleaning and, where necessary, disinfection. <!-- CHECK: 03-hygiene-law --> EHEDG describes five principles—material compatibility, cleanability, drainability, accessibility and segregation—and ISO 14159 covers hygiene requirements for machinery used where consumer risk can arise. <!-- CHECK: 03-ehedg-principles --><!-- CHECK: 03-iso-hygiene --> Even downstream of sealed food, the cell needs sloped stainless surfaces, accessible belt removal, sealed cable entries and documented chemical/temperature limits. An IP67 camera component is not a washdown design. The diagnostic observes the real cleaning method before selecting enclosure, connectors or fasteners.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:101:<p>PackCheck is not a hypothetical future rival. Its Vi-Track material advertises recipe-controlled THT inspection, API control, ejection and a web dashboard; it also publishes a customer account in which Vezet selected the company to inspect film, codes and labels on vegetable bagging lines. <!-- CHECK: 03-packcheck-product --><!-- CHECK: 03-packcheck-case --> Vision Partners’ EVIS covers date, barcode, text and seal checks and connects to labeling software, MES and PLCs. <!-- CHECK: 03-evis-product --> Both are Dutch, local and broader than this proposal.</p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:102:<p>Above them sit the printer vendors. Domino’s R-Series joins printers, cameras and lighting and offers to test a customer’s code on its product before commitment. <!-- CHECK: 03-domino-range-competition --> Videojet-Laetus already offers OCR/OCV, best-before and lot checks, printer synchronisation, SKU management, IP67 camera configurations and diffuse or polarised lighting. <!-- CHECK: 03-videojet-system-competition --> Below them sits the internal automation engineer with a Cognex or Keyence sensor. Taken together, these products cover nearly every functional box in the proposed cell; a generic OCR offer has no credible wedge. <!-- CHECK: 03-incumbent-inference --></p>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:146:  <a href="02-white-label-product-security.html">← Previous</a>
chapters/03-machine-vision-food.html:152:<footer class="site-footer"><div class="wrap"><p>Five candidates. One second engine. Evidence before commitment.</p></div></footer>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:25:<p class="lede">A Dutch machine builder is entering a period in which two obligations attach to one connected product: keep its software secure through a long operating life, and make defined product data accessible on defined terms. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-lifecycle --> <!-- CHECK: 01-data-duty --> The attractive business is not another readiness report. It is the small operating team that keeps product, component, vulnerability, evidence and data-access records true after the report is filed. The fatal possibility is simpler: manufacturers may buy a lawyer, an assessor and a software licence once, then give the recurring queue to existing quality and engineering staff. The first investment decision is therefore not whether the regulation is important. It is whether one narrow manufacturer will pay an outsider to own the queue.</p>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:37:<p>The Cyber Resilience Act applies broadly to products with digital elements made available on the EU market when their intended or reasonably foreseeable use includes a direct or indirect logical or physical data connection. It covers design, development and production requirements, vulnerability-handling processes and market surveillance; machinery can be subject both to the CRA and the Machinery Regulation. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-scope --></p>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:38:<p>The first operational deadline is immediate. From 11 September 2026 manufacturers must report actively exploited vulnerabilities and severe security incidents. An early warning is due within 24 hours, a fuller notification within 72 hours, and the vulnerability final report no later than fourteen days after a corrective or mitigating measure is available. The main CRA obligations apply from 11 December 2027. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-dates --> ENISA says its Single Reporting Platform is the tool manufacturers and CSIRTs will use from that first date. <!-- CHECK: 01-srp --></p>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:39:<p>The longer-lived workload matters more than the filing. A manufacturer must document relevant cybersecurity aspects and vulnerabilities, keep the risk assessment current where appropriate, operate coordinated vulnerability disclosure, and handle vulnerabilities through a support period normally at least five years. The Act specifically notes that industrial products are often used much longer; technical documentation must be continuously updated where appropriate during the support period. <!-- CHECK: 01-cra-lifecycle --> This is the recurring state: product releases alter components; suppliers disclose faults; new vulnerabilities change exploitability; field configurations change exposure; a fix changes evidence; a customer report changes the incident record; support clocks expire.</p>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:40:<p>The Data Act creates a different queue. Its connected-product definition covers an item that obtains, generates or collects use or environmental data and can communicate product data, where storing or transmitting data for someone other than the user is not the primary function. The Commission explicitly gives robots and industrial machinery as examples. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-scope --> Article 3 requires connected products and related services to make product data, related-service data and necessary metadata accessible by default, easily, securely, free of charge and in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable format; pre-contract information must describe the data and access route. Where data are not directly accessible, Article 4 requires the data holder to provide readily available data on a simple electronic request where feasible. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-duty --></p>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:41:<p>The Data Act has applied generally since 12 September 2025, but the Article 3(1) design obligation applies to connected products and related services placed on the market after 12 September 2026. Trade secrets do not create a casual blanket exemption: the Act sets out confidentiality measures, written substantiation and notifications to the competent authority when data are withheld or suspended. <!-- CHECK: 01-data-dates --> <!-- CHECK: 01-data-secrets --> That produces more state changes: a new sensor or firmware build alters the data catalogue; a contract changes permitted use; a user or third party requests access; security or trade-secret controls change the release decision; an interface or retention rule changes.</p>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:43:<p>Regulation proves workload, not paid demand. The best directly relevant field evidence found here is a 2024 qualitative survey of twelve Northern European organisations: seven large industrial-equipment manufacturers and five consultancies or partners, recruited partly through a compliance-automation research project. All surveyed manufacturers already had an IEC 62443-4-1-aligned secure-development lifecycle, so they are not the proposed small customer. <!-- CHECK: 01-survey-sample --> Respondents described ownership, secure-development enforcement, update management, expertise and 24-hour notification as difficult; they found organisational and procedural issues harder than many technical controls. <!-- CHECK: 01-survey-pain --> Yet half expected CRA cost increases below 20%, a quarter expected no cost impact, and half expected employee roles to remain unchanged. The authors explicitly suggest firms may use existing resources. <!-- CHECK: 01-survey-budget --> That is evidence of pain and a warning against assuming a budget. No source here shows a small industrial OEM paying an external operator each month.</p>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:85:<p>ONEKEY already markets firmware/SBOM analysis, vulnerability monitoring, impact assessment and a compliance workflow for manufacturers, combined with consulting. Cybellum offers a product-security platform spanning SBOMs, risk, vulnerabilities, incident response and regulatory evidence. <!-- CHECK: 01-onekey --> <!-- CHECK: 01-cybellum --> The EU-funded CRACoWi project states a value of €8.44 million and a term through August 2027; its wizard is intended to help SMEs, manufacturers, importers and distributors automate CRA assessments and documentation. <!-- CHECK: 01-cracowi --> These are not distant category competitors. They can add service, partner with assessors, lower prices or become the customer's system of record.</p>
chapters/01-connected-product-operations.html:87:<p>Standards will also move the boundary. CEN and CENELEC's 2026 programme says horizontal CRA drafts, vulnerability-handling work and industrial vertical profiles are still being advanced, with key work scheduled for the end of 2026. <!-- CHECK: 01-standards --> Clearer standards help delivery but commoditise the map. The operation must own reliable closure across tools, not proprietary interpretations that disappear when guidance improves.</p>
